/* book exam traps: Two-stroke is not two-cylinder. The label counts piston strokes per cycle, not cylinders; a single-cylinder two-stroke is common. | Diesel is a fuel and a cycle name, while compression ignition is a method. An ignition question wants the words SI or CI. | The history order is Lenoir (1860), Beau de Rochas (1862), Otto (1876), Clerk (about 1880), Diesel (1892 to 1897). Putting Diesel before Clerk, or crediting Otto with the two-stroke, loses the mark. | Supercharged and turbocharged both mean forced induction. A turbocharger is driven by exhaust gas; a supercharger is driven by the crankshaft through a belt or gears. | Air-cooled does not mean uncooled: fins, a fan or the rush of air past a moving motorcycle carry the heat away. */
