/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - These terms describe one cylinder.
   - d is the bore and L the stroke in the same unit; centimetres give cm³.
   - r = (Vs + Vc)/Vc.
   - L is the stroke in m and N the speed in rev/s; the piston covers two strokes per revolution.
   - Mean effective pressure (mep) is the steady pressure that, acting on the piston over one stroke of every cycle, would give the same net work per cycle as the real, changing pressure: mep = Wnet/Vs, with W in J and Vs in m³ giving Pa.
   - Group the parts by job and the list stops feeling long: some hold everything together, some take the gas pressure, some deliver the output, some control the gas flow, and one smooths the rotation.
   - Valve timing and the order in which the cylinders fire are separate topics; see valve and port timing diagrams and firing order, valve layouts and valve actuation.
   - The gap between piston and bore is set for the hot running state, because the aluminium piston expands more than the iron bore.
*/
